  "notes": "The library is plain Rust with no game dependency (see src/smeltery and src/recipes for the style: a struct for the data, `from_json` for the original's generated JSON, plain methods for the behaviour, game questions behind a small trait). Put the port in a new folder src/<name>/ (one file per Java class, or per few small ones) and add one `pub mod <name>;` line to src/lib.rs. Unit tests go in a `#[cfg(test)] mod tests` at the bottom of the file they test, in the existing style: one test per behaviour, written from what the Java does, not from your code; check once that a test fails when the code is broken; no test of getters. For a class that reads the original's data files, add one test to the existing tests/original_data.rs that loads every file of that kind from .local/tconstruct-source (the junction codex_start.py makes) and asserts how many loaded; files that need another mod are skipped through the condition context, as the other tests there do. Mod integration (the plugin package, compat) is not ported. In the inventory, set each class you ported to ported-unchecked with a note of one or two sentences: where the port is, what is tested, what was left."
